1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,760
You step outside and look up.

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:06,105
A second sun has just appeared in the sky.

3
00:00:06,105 --> 00:00:07,894
No warning.

4
00:00:07,894 --> 00:00:09,763
No sound.

5
00:00:09,763 --> 00:00:13,776
Just a blinding white dot, growing larger every second.

6
00:00:13,776 --> 00:00:17,960
Ten seconds pass, and the air around you begins to burn.

7
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:23,184
Sixty-six million years ago, this was the final moment for the dinosaurs.

8
00:00:23,184 --> 00:00:27,236
So what happens if a ten-kilometer rock hits Earth today?

9
00:00:27,236 --> 00:00:33,184
Right now, that asteroid is tearing through the atmosphere at seventy thousand kilometers per hour.

10
00:00:33,184 --> 00:00:39,552
Upon impact, it releases the energy of one billion nuclear bombs in a single millisecond.

11
00:00:39,552 --> 00:00:42,276
Here is the terrifying part.

12
00:00:42,276 --> 00:00:45,986
It is not the blast that kills most of the planet.

13
00:00:45,986 --> 00:00:48,013
It is the dust.

14
00:00:48,013 --> 00:00:54,552
Billions of tons of pulverized rock shoot into the stratosphere, wrapping the Earth in a pitch-black shroud.

15
00:00:54,552 --> 00:00:56,815
Sunlight vanishes.

16
00:00:56,815 --> 00:01:00,328
Global temperatures plunge into a deep freeze.

17
00:01:00,328 --> 00:01:06,750
Without light, photosynthesis stops, plants die, and the entire food chain collapses in months.

18
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:09,302
Now look up at the night sky.

19
00:01:09,302 --> 00:01:16,881
You are alive today only because that ancient rock missed our ancestors, and gave us the time to look back up and watch for the next one.

